FPC1264 trusted application: local-device provisioning only

SHA256.json lists the ten hashes of fpcliu.mdt and fpcliu.b00 through fpcliu.b08
for every stock ROM this product has verified, one set per release:

    OS2.0.6.0.VMYCNXM   Android 15, images/NON-HLOS.bin, FAT directory /image
    OS3.0.7.0.VMYCNXM   Android 15, images/NON-HLOS.bin, FAT directory /image

Two sets exist because Xiaomi re-signed the trusted application between those
releases, and a tablet's modem_a carries whichever one its last stock update
wrote. The two differ only in fpcliu.mdt (the ELF header and program headers)
and fpcliu.b08 (the signature); the eight b00-b07 segments are byte-identical.

A device is accepted only when it matches ONE set in full. Selecting a set per
file would let a unit that matches no release be assembled out of two, giving an
image whose header and signature do not describe its body. A unit matching no
set disables the fingerprint service, exactly as before; holding several
identities widens which stock images are accepted and does not make the check
advisory.

Adding a release is a deliberate act. Its ten hashes go into SHA256.json with
the ROM version and the source they were read from, and every hash must be
reproduced from that ROM's NON-HLOS.bin rather than copied off a device.

These OEM binaries are not included in the public liuqin-fingerprint package;
the project has not established permission to redistribute them. On the first
normal boot, provision_firmware.py mounts this tablet's modem_a read-only,
chooses the identity the mounted image matches as a whole, and copies only these
ten files after verifying every hash. Subsequent boots reuse the verified local
cache. A missing or unrecognised stock image disables the fingerprint service
without modifying modem_a or preventing password login.

The files are generic OEM trusted applications, not fingerprints, enrollment
credentials, device calibration or Android user data. This package creates no
biometric identity during build/install/firmware provisioning. A root-private
random credential is created only on a Polkit-authorized native enrollment.
